menuconfig TORABO_CAPS
    bool "Torabo capability descriptor (firmware self-description)"
    default n
    help
      Expose "what can this firmware do?" to Torabo-Studio: the firmware version,
      which feature modules were compiled in, each one's wire version, and
      per-feature capability bits.

      A build is assembled from snippets, so two keyboards running the same app can
      have completely different feature sets. Rather than have the app guess (or
      probe every GATT service and see which ones fail), the firmware names itself
      once on connect and the app shows only the tabs that exist.

      Enable on the CENTRAL — it is the half the app talks to.

if TORABO_CAPS

module = TORABO_CAPS

# BLE detail (was CONFIG_TORABO_CAPS_BLE's own help; kept here since Kconfig
# help text can't take a $(var) — see features/common/Kconfig.template.torabo_ble):
#   Read-only GATT service e1f4a000. Nothing to write: it describes the build.
torabo-thing = "the descriptor"
rsource "../common/Kconfig.template.torabo_ble"

# TUNNEL detail (was CONFIG_TORABO_CAPS_TUNNEL's own help in full — this one
# never fit the other 7 features' shared "Register the settings..." shape):
#   Same read-only descriptor as the GATT service (feature id 0x00), reachable
#   over whichever transport Studio RPC is using. This is how a USB-connected
#   app discovers that the tunnel exists at all, so keep it on wherever the
#   tunnel is on: the descriptor gains an RpcTunnel entry when it is.
torabo-thing = "the descriptor"
rsource "../common/Kconfig.template.torabo_tunnel"

config TORABO_FW_VERSION_MAJOR
    int "Torabo firmware version: major"
    default 0

config TORABO_FW_VERSION_MINOR
    int "Torabo firmware version: minor"
    default 1

config TORABO_FW_VERSION_PATCH
    int "Torabo firmware version: patch"
    default 1

config TORABO_CENTRAL_SIDE
    int "Which physical half is the central (0=unknown/1=left/2=right)"
    default 0
    help
      Purely descriptive (PLAN-ext-fw-refactor.md phase 9): reported in the caps
      descriptor header's `_rsv` byte so Torabo-Studio's FW info tab can show
      which half is central without depending on the LED feature's
      CENTRAL_IS_LEFT bit, which simply doesn't exist in an LED-less build.
      Doesn't affect any runtime behavior — the firmware builder sets this from
      the hardware pattern the user picked. Leave at 0 (unknown) if unsure; the
      app falls back to its existing guess.

config TORABO_SLOT_LEFT_STD
    int "What is plugged into the left half's standard connector (0=undeclared/1=ball/2=pad/3=4-way switch/4=dial/9=encoder/15=none)"
    range 0 15
    default 0
    help
      Purely descriptive (PLAN-ext-fw-refactor.md phase 9, re-redesigned
      2026-09-03 into one 4-bit-per-slot declaration — this is that redesign's
      whole point: one Kconfig, one caps nibble, one physical connector, no
      per-feature bit spreading). Reported in the TORABO_FEAT_MODULES caps row
      so Torabo-Studio's FW info tab can show exactly what is on this slot
      without guessing. Doesn't affect any runtime behavior. 0 = undeclared
      (the default; also what every conf that never sets this reports, so an
      old build and a build that genuinely doesn't know look the same — both
      fall back to Studio's existing process-of-elimination guess). 1 = a
      trackball, 2 = a mini-trackpad, 3 = a 4-way switch module (reserved; the
      builder does not offer it yet), 4 = a high-resolution dial, 9 = a rotary
      encoder (a self-made module, hence the gap), 15 = explicitly nothing
      here. These numbers are independent of the trackpad TP_META kind
      numbering; an app that needs both keeps its own mapping table. Set by the
      firmware builder from the hardware pattern the user picked; leave at 0 if
      unsure.

config TORABO_SLOT_LEFT_EXT
    int "What is plugged into the left half's extension connector (0=undeclared/1=ball/2=pad/3=4-way switch/4=dial/9=encoder/15=none)"
    range 0 15
    default 0
    help
      Same meaning as TORABO_SLOT_LEFT_STD, for the left half's extension
      connector instead of its standard connector. A ball never appears here:
      the extension FFC carries no SPI, so a ball is always on a half's own
      standard connector — the firmware builder never sets this to 1, but the
      range still allows it since this is a plain descriptive int, not a
      hardware interlock.

config TORABO_SLOT_RIGHT_STD
    int "What is plugged into the right half's standard connector (0=undeclared/1=ball/2=pad/3=4-way switch/4=dial/9=encoder/15=none)"
    range 0 15
    default 0
    help
      Same meaning as TORABO_SLOT_LEFT_STD, for the right half's standard
      connector.

config TORABO_SLOT_RIGHT_EXT
    int "What is plugged into the right half's extension connector (0=undeclared/1=ball/2=pad/3=4-way switch/4=dial/9=encoder/15=none)"
    range 0 15
    default 0
    help
      Same meaning as TORABO_SLOT_LEFT_EXT, for the right half's extension
      connector (no ball here either, for the same hardware reason).

module-str = Torabo Caps
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # TORABO_CAPS
